※ pl4g-test: compile=fail
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4021 LANG_TAKE_RUN_NOT_A_VALUE
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4021 LANG_TAKE_RUN_NOT_A_VALUE
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4021 LANG_TAKE_RUN_NOT_A_VALUE

※ `†l⟦i⟧` answers the element it took out; `†l⟦i…j⟧` answers nothing.  The run
※ is not kept anywhere an answer could be made of, and a list of it would be
※ room made for something a program cutting a list short almost never reads --
※ so it is a statement and only a statement.  Bound to a name, an operand of an
※ operator, a function's answer: each is a value wanted of it.  A program that
※ wants the elements reads them first, `l⟦i…j⟧`, and copies what it keeps.

let std := ⎕import("std")

fn cut(l: mut [u8]) → [u8]:
    †l⟦0…1⟧

@[startup]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    let l: mut [u8] = [1u8, 2u8, 3u8]
    let gone := †l⟦0…1⟧
    let n: u64 = #(†l⟦0…1⟧)
    0u6
